{
  "schema": "verify-select-backtest-1.0",
  "note": [
    "ADR-0068 Decision 5, item 4: pull requests whose first full-tests run failed, with the test files that failed in it.",
    "Collected 2026-10-20 from the full-tests runs GitHub kept (runs from 2026-06-07 on), release-please pull requests and runs that failed with no failing test (a timeout) left out.",
    "Each entry is replayed from main's history, not from the failing run's commit, which a squash merge leaves unreachable: the change is base..head, where head is the merge GitHub recorded and base the commit before the pull request's commits on main (head^ for a squash, head~k for a rebase merge of k commits). The selection reads the final change, an approximation of the first run's.",
    "An entry's mappings are the declaration mappings its replay adds: a test that failed on a path no rule sees.",
    "Where the failing run's commit (run_sha) is in the clone, the backtest also replays it against its fork point with the change's head: the first run's own tree. Most of these commits are on no branch GitHub keeps, so CI's clone lacks them and replays the merge only.",
    "selected is the number of tests the rules (not the fallback) selected on the merge replay when recorded, 2026-10-21: a ceiling the backtest holds, so a rule that widens shows here and raising it is a deliberate edit.",
    "fallback_only names the failing tests no rule selects, with why: each passes only because its change selects the whole suite, which the backtest asserts. A rule that comes to select one fails the backtest until the entry drops it.",
    "#744 records a docs/** mapping (2026-10-21): its doc-version test failed on main's documents against the release tags, not on the ADRs the change edits, so no reading rule selects it; the suite caught it only while no test read an ADR. tests/scripts/test-set-terminal-archive-timing.mjs walks every document, so a rule now matches the ADRs and the fallback no longer fires (ADR-0068 D5 item 4: a match cancels it); #710 records the same test by mapping."
  ],
